WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:07.100
This is the rapid-fire track: every answer in this episode, back to back, one breath each.

2
00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:09.755
Made for the night before the Pharm final.

3
00:00:09.755 --> 00:00:13.549
If one of these sounds unfamiliar, go back to its own track.

4
00:00:14.999 --> 00:00:16.108
<v Examiner>Licit and illicit.

5
00:00:16.558 --> 00:00:20.482
Licit means lawful and permitted: prescription, restricted,

6
00:00:20.482 --> 00:00:26.268
pharmacy-only and general sale medicines, and some controlled drugs from Class B and C.

7
00:00:26.268 --> 00:00:29.062
Alcohol, or an opioid taken as prescribed.

8
00:00:29.062 --> 00:00:34.981
Illicit means unlawful or not permitted: every Class A drug, some Class B, a few Class C.

9
00:00:34.981 --> 00:00:36.644
Methamphetamine, Class A.

10
00:00:36.644 --> 00:00:39.171
Controlled is not the same as illicit.

11
00:00:40.421 --> 00:00:41.637
<v Examiner>Misuse and abuse.

12
00:00:42.087 --> 00:00:47.759
Misuse: any substance used for a purpose different from legal or medical guidelines.

13
00:00:47.759 --> 00:00:49.919
In New Zealand law, any illegal,

14
00:00:49.919 --> 00:00:55.253
harmful or unauthorised use of a controlled drug under the Misuse of Drugs Act.

15
00:00:55.253 --> 00:00:57.616
Taking someone else's prescription.

16
00:00:57.616 --> 00:01:01.532
Abuse: self-administered in chronically excessive amounts,

17
00:01:01.532 --> 00:01:07.271
outside approved medical use or social norms, causing physical or psychological harm.

18
00:01:07.271 --> 00:01:10.512
Drinking heavily every day until it does damage.

19
00:01:11.762 --> 00:01:12.658
<v Examiner>Dependence.

20
00:01:13.108 --> 00:01:18.884
Dependence: a drug pursued compulsively, with no medical need, despite adverse effects.

21
00:01:18.884 --> 00:01:23.599
Psychological dependence is out-of-control cravings, often with denial.

22
00:01:23.599 --> 00:01:28.711
Physical dependence is withdrawal symptoms when the drug isn't used, her key:

23
00:01:28.711 --> 00:01:31.433
if a prescribed drug is stopped abruptly.

24
00:01:31.433 --> 00:01:36.878
An opioid taken for months and stopped suddenly causes withdrawal, so it's weaned.

25
00:01:36.878 --> 00:01:41.127
Insulin or a daily inhaler is medical dependence, not addiction.

26
00:01:42.377 --> 00:01:43.124
<v Examiner>Addiction.

27
00:01:43.574 --> 00:01:46.414
Addiction: a behavioural pattern of drug use,

28
00:01:46.414 --> 00:01:50.202
with overwhelming involvement in getting and using the drug.

29
00:01:50.202 --> 00:01:55.567
It impairs motivation-reward, mood and behavioural inhibition, and relapse is likely.

30
00:01:55.567 --> 00:01:58.660
Someone who keeps using despite losing their job.

31
00:01:58.660 --> 00:02:03.394
The difference: addiction is craving, loss of control and use despite harm.

32
00:02:03.394 --> 00:02:06.550
Dependence is withdrawal when the drug is stopped.

33
00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:09.187
<v Examiner>Tolerance and withdrawal.

34
00:02:09.637 --> 00:02:13.117
Tolerance: repeated doses give decreasing effects,

35
00:02:13.117 --> 00:02:18.546
so the dose has to go up for the same effect, through receptor downregulation.

36
00:02:18.546 --> 00:02:21.609
Morphine, rapidly; marijuana, hardly at all.

37
00:02:21.609 --> 00:02:26.482
Withdrawal: the symptoms when a dependent person stops, like insomnia,

38
00:02:26.482 --> 00:02:31.354
irritability and cravings, worse when stopped cold turkey than weaned.

39
00:02:31.354 --> 00:02:36.993
Substance use disorder is diagnosed with the DSM 5, by how many criteria are met:

40
00:02:36.993 --> 00:02:38.872
more criteria, more severe.

41
00:02:40.122 --> 00:02:41.530
<v Examiner>Stimulant or depressant.

42
00:02:41.980 --> 00:02:46.401
Stimulants speed up the body and brain: MDMA, methamphetamine.

43
00:02:46.401 --> 00:02:51.963
Depressants slow them down: alcohol, GHB, sedatives, nitrous oxide, inhalants.

44
00:02:51.963 --> 00:02:57.169
Some are both: nicotine, marijuana, psychedelics, synthetic cannabinoids.

45
00:02:57.169 --> 00:03:02.588
Two depressants together, like alcohol and an opioid, are the dangerous mix.

46
00:03:03.838 --> 00:03:04.905
<v Examiner>Risk factors.

47
00:03:05.355 --> 00:03:10.611
Four groups of risk factors for substance abuse in young adults: family-related,

48
00:03:10.611 --> 00:03:14.224
like relatives who use or childhood trauma; individual,

49
00:03:14.224 --> 00:03:19.284
like an impulsive personality or mental ill health; social and environmental,

50
00:03:19.284 --> 00:03:23.291
like peer influence and societal acceptance; and behavioural,

51
00:03:23.291 --> 00:03:25.919
like frequent use that started socially.

52
00:03:25.919 --> 00:03:28.745
Anyone can take drugs, so don't stereotype.

53
00:03:29.995 --> 00:03:30.976
<v Examiner>Interactions.

54
00:03:31.426 --> 00:03:36.400
A medication-drug interaction: a licit or illicit substance, like alcohol,

55
00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:41.777
marijuana or nicotine, changes a medicine's effect, or together they cause harm.

56
00:03:41.777 --> 00:03:45.474
Check the New Zealand Formulary's Interactions checker.

57
00:03:45.474 --> 00:03:50.045
Alcohol with another depressant, like an opioid, or benzodiazepines,

58
00:03:50.045 --> 00:03:51.927
raises the risk of overdose.

59
00:03:53.177 --> 00:03:54.223
<v Examiner>The nurse's role.

60
00:03:54.673 --> 00:03:59.950
The nurse's role: don't stereotype; ask about use, give a brief intervention,

61
00:03:59.950 --> 00:04:02.760
offer referrals, follow up, and advocate.

62
00:04:02.760 --> 00:04:08.928
Refer to Community Alcohol and Drug Services: Te Ātea Marinō for Māori, Tupu for Pasifika,

63
00:04:08.928 --> 00:04:13.246
Altered High for youth, or a rehab provider like Odyssey House.

64
00:04:13.246 --> 00:04:19.209
Harm reduction: make use safer for people who will use anyway, like free drug checking,

65
00:04:19.209 --> 00:04:22.841
or naloxone for people at risk of an opioid overdose.

66
00:04:24.091 --> 00:04:24.945
<v Examiner>Poisoning.

67
00:04:25.395 --> 00:04:30.221
Poisoning: harm from a substance swallowed or touched, usually accidental,

68
00:04:30.221 --> 00:04:34.395
and almost anything can poison, depending on dose, age and size.

69
00:04:34.395 --> 00:04:39.156
Acute is all at once, like a child swallowing cleaner; chronic builds up,

70
00:04:39.156 --> 00:04:40.330
like heavy metals.

71
00:04:40.330 --> 00:04:44.569
Poisonous, you eat or touch it; venomous, it bites or stings you.

72
00:04:44.569 --> 00:04:49.721
The National Poisons Centre: oh eight hundred, poison, free, twenty-four hours,

73
00:04:49.721 --> 00:04:55.069
for swallowed products, overdoses, fumes, splashes and recreational drug symptoms.

74
00:04:55.069 --> 00:04:57.809
Unconscious or not breathing: one one one.

75
00:04:59.059 --> 00:04:59.912
<v Examiner>Antidotes.

76
00:05:00.362 --> 00:05:05.670
An antidote counteracts a poison: by binding it, like activated charcoal;

77
00:05:05.670 --> 00:05:11.560
by slowing or speeding an enzyme; or by blocking its receptors, as an antagonist.

78
00:05:11.560 --> 00:05:17.014
Naloxone, Narcan, is an opioid antagonist that reverses an opioid overdose.

79
00:05:17.014 --> 00:05:22.613
N-acetylcysteine, N A C, reduces the toxic metabolites made from paracetamol.

80
00:05:23.863 --> 00:05:24.887
<v Examiner>Her formatives.

81
00:05:25.337 --> 00:05:30.159
Dependence is withdrawal symptoms if a prescribed drug is stopped abruptly,

82
00:05:30.159 --> 00:05:31.510
her corrected key, B.

83
00:05:31.510 --> 00:05:33.760
Addiction is a behavioural pattern,

84
00:05:33.760 --> 00:05:37.618
with overwhelming involvement in getting and using the drug.

85
00:05:37.618 --> 00:05:42.569
Controlled drugs are under special legal controls to reduce misuse and abuse,

86
00:05:42.569 --> 00:05:44.305
and they can be prescribed.

87
00:05:44.305 --> 00:05:50.028
And a half dose given by mistake is a medication error, not a call to the Poisons Centre.

88
00:05:51.278 --> 00:05:52.416
That's every answer.

89
00:05:52.416 --> 00:05:53.326
Go and get them.
